# Seeker Project Rules

## Phase boundary

Phase 2 (current) is the generic game engine, now built (`engine/`, `lib/game/`, `app/play/`) and validated by a non-canonical `content/demo/` pack. Real Kindle Life chapter content lands next, chapter by chapter, as new `content/<packId>/` packs. Do NOT add auth, teacher dashboards, or other "Later" platform features unless the task explicitly asks for that work. Read `docs/VISION.md` and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` for product context.

## Stack (do not swap without instruction)

Next.js App Router + TypeScript + Tailwind at repo root, Prisma 6 + Postgres (Neon in prod, Docker Compose locally), Vercel Blob (object storage), Vitest, Playwright, GitHub Actions, pnpm.

## Quality gates

- Every change must pass `pnpm lint && pnpm typecheck && pnpm test` before it is considered done.
- Changes to pages or API routes must also pass `pnpm test:e2e` (requires local Postgres: `docker compose up -d`).
- Database schema changes go through Prisma migrations (`pnpm db:migrate`); never edit the database or migration history by hand.
- Never weaken, skip, or delete CI checks to make a failing build pass.

## Code style

- Server Components by default; add `"use client"` only when interactivity requires it.
- Strong typing everywhere; no `any` unless unavoidable and commented.
- Small composable modules; shared logic lives in `lib/`.
- No secrets in the repo. `DATABASE_URL` and `BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN` come from `.env` / `.env.local` locally (gitignored), GitHub secrets in CI where needed, Vercel env vars in deploys. See `docs/runbooks/storage.md`.

## Three-layer boundary

`engine/` stays generic (no book-specific logic, no Prisma/cookie imports), `content/<packId>/` is pure data (no business logic), platform concerns (`lib/game/`, `app/play/`) stay out of both.
